meta {
  name: 9g4. Verify open data is universal — anonymous OWS payload (no token → 200)
  type: http
  seq: 28
  tags: [
    requires-nifi
  ]
}

get {
  url: {{apisixApiUrl}}/v1/datasets/{{sagaDataSetId}}/{{sagaOwsSlug}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

script:pre-request {
  // The dataset carries TWO named APIs: STA (things, FROST upstream) and OWS (map, GeoServer
  // upstream). 9g already proved the STA payload opens anonymously; this proves the SAME OPA
  // open-data grant applies to the OWS route, i.e. open data is universal across routable backends
  // (the rule is standard-agnostic — every dataset route carries the same service_id, so OPA
  // dispatches both to the path-based frost_server policy). Poll the OWS data-plane WITHOUT a token
  // until 200, max ~50s (25 × 2s), to absorb propagation (DB commit + OPA fetch). While protected
  // the gateway answers 401; QuickJS axios throws on non-2xx, so catch and read the status.
  const axios = require('axios');
  // Resolve the OWS slug once and pin it so the request URL ({{sagaOwsSlug}}) and this warm-up loop
  // hit the SAME endpoint even if the variable was not set upstream.
  const slug = bru.getVar('sagaOwsSlug') || 'map';
  bru.setVar('sagaOwsSlug', slug);
  const url = `${bru.getEnvVar('apisixApiUrl')}/v1/datasets/${bru.getVar('sagaDataSetId')}/${slug}`;
  for (let i = 0; i < 25; i++) {
    let status = 0;
    try { status = (await axios.get(url)).status; }
    catch (e) { status = e.response ? e.response.status : 0; }
    if (status === 200) break;
    await new Promise(res => setTimeout(res, 2000));
  }
}

tests {
  test("Anonymous OWS payload of an open dataset is reachable through the gateway (200)", function() {
    // 200 proves the anonymous request traversed APISIX → OIDC (unauth_action: pass) → OPA
    // (open-data ABAC grant) → the OWS (GeoServer) upstream end-to-end — not a FROST-only path.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("Response came from the OWS (GeoServer) upstream — echoed path is the OWS rewrite", function() {
    // The geoserver-mock stub echoes the request it received. The OWS route's proxy-rewrite maps
    // /v1/datasets/{id}/map → /geoserver/{workspace}/ows, so the echoed `path` proves the request
    // actually reached the GeoServer/OWS upstream (a FROST hit would echo /FROST-Server/...).
    expect(res.body).to.be.an('object');
    expect(res.body.path, 'echoed upstream path').to.be.a('string');
    expect(res.body.path).to.match(/^\/geoserver\/.+\/ows(\/|\?|$)/);
  });
}

docs {
  **Saga step 9g4** — Open Data Access is **universal across routable backends (STA + OWS)**.

  The saga dataset publishes two named APIs: `things` (STA → FROST upstream) and `map` (OWS → GeoServer
  upstream). 9g proved the STA payload opens anonymously; this step proves the **OWS** payload does too,
  via the **same** OPA open-data decision. It is the direct evidence for the acceptance criterion
  "anonymous GET to an open dataset's payload (STA **and** OWS) returns 200": the request goes through
  APISIX → OIDC (`unauth_action: pass`) → OPA (open-data ABAC) → the OWS upstream and returns 200.

  The OWS upstream is a lightweight HTTP echo stub (`geoserver-mock`) standing in for GeoServer — an OWS
  named API provisions only the APISIX route to `apisix.geoserver.url`, not a GeoServer workspace, so no
  real GeoServer is needed to exercise the gateway/authz chain.

  **Precondition:** runs while the dataset is open (after 9f, before 9h); pre-request polled until 200.

  **Expected Response:** 200 OK from the OWS upstream — no bearer token sent.
}
